WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.370
Instructor: Okay, so we will soon learn

00:02.370 --> 00:05.010
what git stashing is in this particular commands.

00:05.010 --> 00:08.220
In this video, I want to explain why it's a thing.

00:08.220 --> 00:12.720
What is the problem that git stashing or git stash solves?

00:12.720 --> 00:15.600
So imagine for just a moment here

00:15.600 --> 00:19.800
we're working on a repository and I'm on the master branch.

00:19.800 --> 00:21.807
You can see up top on the right side, I've

00:21.807 --> 00:23.730
I've got a single file I'm working on.

00:23.730 --> 00:26.580
A Python file called cat.py.

00:26.580 --> 00:27.720
So I'm doing a whole bunch

00:27.720 --> 00:30.750
of work and then I make some commits

00:30.750 --> 00:33.030
and then I switch over to a new branch.

00:33.030 --> 00:34.140
I just make that new branch.

00:34.140 --> 00:36.120
I switch to it, it's called puppy

00:36.120 --> 00:38.403
and I do some more work over here.

00:39.240 --> 00:42.840
And then I have some uncommitted work on this puppy branch.

00:42.840 --> 00:46.110
So I never committed this dog file, for example.

00:46.110 --> 00:47.940
And then I need to switch back to master.

00:47.940 --> 00:49.290
I don't know why, something's wrong,

00:49.290 --> 00:51.000
I need to switch back quickly.

00:51.000 --> 00:54.540
What happens if I have a file or a hundred files,

00:54.540 --> 00:56.550
if I have uncommitted changes

00:56.550 --> 01:00.090
on one branch and I switch to another branch?

01:00.090 --> 01:02.130
Well, there's really two options.

01:02.130 --> 01:04.770
One is that those changes will just come

01:04.770 --> 01:07.050
with me to the, to the destination branch.

01:07.050 --> 01:08.730
They'll just come along and show

01:08.730 --> 01:11.340
up on the master branch in this case.

01:11.340 --> 01:14.070
The other option is that git won't let me switch.

01:14.070 --> 01:16.740
And I actually showed this early on when I first

01:16.740 --> 01:18.725
introed branching, git will give you a message

01:18.725 --> 01:20.970
if there's a conflict between files,

01:20.970 --> 01:22.770
it's not gonna let you just switch

01:22.770 --> 01:25.680
to a new branch and bring that conflict over.

01:25.680 --> 01:28.170
So I'm gonna show this in a very simple way.

01:28.170 --> 01:29.670
I'm gonna try and illustrate it here

01:29.670 --> 01:32.760
with some HTML and some CSS in a new git repo.

01:32.760 --> 01:35.700
But this is a problem or it could be a problem.

01:35.700 --> 01:37.410
There are lots of times you might be working

01:37.410 --> 01:38.880
on something but you're not done.

01:38.880 --> 01:41.460
You don't wanna make an actual official commit

01:41.460 --> 01:42.780
but you need to go somewhere else.

01:42.780 --> 01:44.310
You need to go to a different branch.

01:44.310 --> 01:46.140
A coworker asks for help.

01:46.140 --> 01:48.240
You realize that there's a really simple typo

01:48.240 --> 01:50.040
that you could fix in a different branch.

01:50.040 --> 01:51.990
You don't need to wait until you make a commit

01:51.990 --> 01:55.740
on your current branch because we can stash changes.

01:55.740 --> 01:59.160
So let me show you a situation where this would be useful.

01:59.160 --> 02:02.610
I am in a new folder it's just called stashing,

02:02.610 --> 02:05.083
just a little demo. It's not a repo at the moment.

02:05.083 --> 02:07.440
I am going to make it one

02:07.440 --> 02:12.420
and I'm gonna make an index HTML file and an app.css file.

02:12.420 --> 02:16.831
So if you don't know CSS and HTML, it's totally fine.

02:16.831 --> 02:19.275
I'm just gonna illustrate this here.

02:19.275 --> 02:22.200
I'm gonna make a simple HTML file.

02:22.200 --> 02:24.900
I'll just call it 'hello.'

02:24.900 --> 02:26.220
I'll have an h1 one in here.

02:26.220 --> 02:28.110
A heading 'Hello World.'

02:28.110 --> 02:31.620
Again, the HTML is not the point of this video.

02:31.620 --> 02:34.300
And I'll add some lorem ipsum in a paragraph.

02:34.300 --> 02:38.523
And then I'm gonna connect this app.css file.

02:39.540 --> 02:42.423
So I'll just do a link app.css.

02:43.443 --> 02:45.300
Okay, so nothing crazy.

02:45.300 --> 02:48.810
I have an empty CSS file and if I view my file

02:48.810 --> 02:50.880
in the browser, this is what it looks like right now.

02:50.880 --> 02:54.450
And let's just say that's all I want on the master branch.

02:54.450 --> 02:56.460
So I'm gonna add and commit,

02:56.460 --> 02:58.710
git add, git commit everything.

02:58.710 --> 03:02.400
Okay? Create index html and app.css.

03:02.400 --> 03:05.010
Okay, so this is done on the master branch.

03:05.010 --> 03:07.050
Now I create a new branch.

03:07.050 --> 03:10.350
So git switch, let's just do it dash with C.

03:10.350 --> 03:12.120
And I'm gonna call this branch something

03:12.120 --> 03:14.505
like purple for example.

03:14.505 --> 03:16.590
And I'll show you why in a moment.

03:16.590 --> 03:19.110
I'm gonna work on making a purple version

03:19.110 --> 03:23.400
of this website where I add some changes into app.css

03:23.400 --> 03:24.960
that will make my website purple.

03:24.960 --> 03:29.960
So I'll do things like h1 should have a color of purple.

03:30.330 --> 03:32.820
I'll save, I refresh. And there it is.

03:32.820 --> 03:34.138
We have some purple there.

03:34.138 --> 03:37.383
Maybe I'll do, the body has a purple background color.

03:38.250 --> 03:42.180
Background color should be, let's not do purple.

03:42.180 --> 03:43.470
Let's do, is there

03:43.470 --> 03:46.440
like a light purpley color that I can just name?

03:46.440 --> 03:49.230
Okay, I chose lavender. Okay, I don't know

03:49.230 --> 03:51.150
if that's gonna show up great on a screen recording

03:51.150 --> 03:53.550
but you can see I've made some changes here.

03:53.550 --> 03:55.380
We have some purpley stuff.

03:55.380 --> 03:58.350
However, I'm not going to commit, for whatever reason

03:58.350 --> 04:00.300
I need to go back to master for a moment.

04:00.300 --> 04:03.450
I've been called away and I'm gonna switch.

04:03.450 --> 04:06.600
So right now, if I type git status, we can see it says,

04:06.600 --> 04:09.180
oh you have some changes in app.css.

04:09.180 --> 04:10.680
They're not staged for commits.

04:10.680 --> 04:12.270
There's no commits on this branch.

04:12.270 --> 04:14.250
If I switch right now to master,

04:14.250 --> 04:19.250
git switch master, hmm no problem.

04:20.070 --> 04:21.090
I didn't get a warning

04:21.090 --> 04:23.670
or I didn't get an alert that says you can't do that.

04:23.670 --> 04:28.470
I'm on master right now but I still see this purple webpage.

04:28.470 --> 04:30.510
My changes came with me.

04:30.510 --> 04:32.280
So that was the first scenario here.

04:32.280 --> 04:34.980
If I make changes and they're not committed,

04:34.980 --> 04:36.090
they can come with me

04:36.090 --> 04:38.700
to the destination branch, assuming they don't conflict.

04:38.700 --> 04:40.980
And I don't have any conflicts here, right?

04:40.980 --> 04:43.650
This is basically a, if I was merging

04:43.650 --> 04:45.020
it would be a fast forward.

04:45.020 --> 04:47.610
But anyway, nothing has been added or committed.

04:47.610 --> 04:51.570
And you'll see if I typed git status, it still says

04:51.570 --> 04:55.110
you know, those changes are not staged for commit right now.

04:55.110 --> 04:57.570
Okay, so that's one option.

04:57.570 --> 05:00.899
If I go back to purple, git switch purple,

05:00.899 --> 05:03.000
I can add those changes

05:03.000 --> 05:07.593
and you know, git commit, make stuff purple.

05:09.960 --> 05:13.900
Okay? And then I'll go back to master, git switch master

05:15.330 --> 05:16.950
and now those changes are gone.

05:16.950 --> 05:20.070
They didn't come with me because I added, I committed them

05:20.070 --> 05:24.270
over on the purple branch and then I switched over.

05:24.270 --> 05:27.480
They didn't come with me. Great. Okay, so next step

05:27.480 --> 05:30.720
I'm gonna show this second scenario, which is also common.

05:30.720 --> 05:32.850
If git detects potential conflicts

05:32.850 --> 05:34.470
it's not going to be happy with us.

05:34.470 --> 05:36.030
It won't let us switch branches

05:36.030 --> 05:38.392
if we haven't added and committed those changes.

05:38.392 --> 05:42.030
So I'm gonna make a new branch here, git switch dash C

05:42.030 --> 05:45.690
I'm on master, and this new branch, let's see

05:45.690 --> 05:47.550
maybe I'll change the name of our webpage.

05:47.550 --> 05:49.530
